# SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
# Counterfactual: a seventeenth vocabulary rule admitting an unratified payer kind
#
# The 2026-09-09 money-and-influence ruling names six payer kinds, and the
# sixth — ControllingPartyPayer — is the shell-actor test: where the nominal
# payer is controlled by another, the controlling party is what the attesters
# must name. One added line names an anonymous intermediary as a seventh kind,
# and it holds membership the moment anyone observes it. That is the exact
# widening that would hollow out the shell test, since a finding could then
# satisfy the payer dimension by naming nobody. The assertion surface
# reconciles relations rather than heads and would not see it;
# `src/checks/repository.rs` is what refuses it.
#
# What this fixture deliberately does NOT show is a finding turning on the new
# kind. The political-finance rule names its sixteen constants literally rather
# than through a variable, so a seventeenth member changes nothing a query can
# reach; the harm here is that a legal category widened by one line.
:expect-pins 2

observe(FinanceWidenSource, FinanceWidenRecord, AnonymousIntermediaryPayer, PoliticalFinancePayerKindScope).

# The vocabulary grew: an unratified payer kind now holds membership.
? member(AnonymousIntermediaryPayer, PoliticalFinancePayerKindVocabulary).
# => TRUE
# The control that keeps the line above from being vacuous: membership still
# requires its own ground rule, so an unobserved token holds nothing.
? member(ControllingPartyPayer, PoliticalFinancePayerKindVocabulary).
# => FALSE
